.docname {Settings}
.include {docs}

Open the **Settings** dialog with the gear button near the right end of the toolbar. It has five sections: Board, Simulation, Randomization, Display, and Developer. The dialog scrolls when it does not fit, with a scrollbar on the right, and messages about your changes appear below it.

Choices that are on or off use switches, choices between options use joined buttons where the current one is highlighted, and every text field that needs confirming has an **Apply** button at the end of its row.

## Board

Pick a preset (128², 256², 512², or 1024²) to fill in the size, or type a **Width** and **Height** in cells, then click **Apply**. Each side can be from 8 to 2048 cells. Anything else is rejected with a message, and the board keeps its size.

Resizing keeps the cells that fit in the new size, fits the board into view, and clears the [undo history](editing.qd#history-limits).

### Fill canvas

The **Fill canvas** switch is on by default. It sizes the board to the canvas, so **Fit board** shows it edge to edge at 100% with no empty border. While it is on, the board follows the canvas: resizing the window resizes the board too. The palette floats over the canvas, so it has no effect on the board.

> Warning: Like any resize, a canvas change keeps only the cells that fit and clears the undo history. Shrinking the window removes cells beyond the new right and top edges.

Switch it off to keep the current size from then on; with it off, resizing the window only changes how much of the board you can see. Applying a size also switches it off. Settings files saved before Fill canvas existed keep their saved size.

## Simulation

- **Rule:** pick **Life**, **HighLife**, **Day & Night**, or **Seeds**, or type a rulestring and click **Apply**. See [Rules](simulation.qd#rules) for the notation.
- **Edges:** choose **Wrap** or **Fixed**. See [Edges](simulation.qd#edges).
- **Pause when settled** pauses playback once the board stops changing or starts repeating. It is off by default. See [When the board settles](simulation.qd#when-the-board-settles).
- **Reset generation counter** sets the generation back to 0 without touching the cells.

## Randomization

Drag the **Density** slider, or focus it and use the arrow keys, to choose 0–100%. It applies as it moves. Optionally enter a **Seed** from 0 to 18446744073709551615 and click **Apply**; a blank seed means a fresh board each time. The default is 25% with no seed. The same seed, density, and board dimensions reproduce the same starting layout; share all three to repeat an experiment. Use the same rule and edge mode to reproduce its evolution.

The toolbar's Randomize button and .keybinding {R} use these preferences. **Randomize now** applies the entered seed and replaces the board immediately as one undoable edit. An invalid seed leaves both the preferences and the board unchanged.

Density is a probability per cell, so the population is approximate. At 0% the board
is empty; at 100% it is full. Rule, edges, and generation counter stay as they are.
Preferences are saved between runs and included in workspace files. Older files default
to 25% with a blank seed.

## Display

- **Theme:** pick **Dark**, **Light**, or **High contrast** to change the colours of the board, the pattern thumbnails, and the toolbar, palette, status bar, and dialogs. **Light** shows dark green cells on a white board. **High contrast** uses black panels, white text, bright green cells, yellow fixed edges, and a yellow focus outline.
- **Grid** shows or hides cell lines.
- **Cell age** shades cells by age and leaves fading trails where cells died. See [Cell age](interface.qd#cell-age).

## Developer

**Show diagnostics** opens the [diagnostics view](interface.qd#diagnostics), which .keybinding {F3} also toggles.

## What is saved

These settings are saved whenever they change, and restored on the next launch:

| Setting        | Saved as                             |
|----------------|--------------------------------------|
| Board size     | `board_cols`, `board_rows`           |
| Fill canvas    | `fill_canvas`                        |
| Grid           | `grid`                               |
| Cell age       | `cell_age`                           |
| Theme          | `theme`                              |
| Rule           | `rule`                               |
| Edge mode      | `boundary_mode`                      |
| Speed          | `tick_rate_ms`, `max_speed`          |
| Pause when settled | `pause_when_settled`             |
| Randomization  | `random_density_percent`, `random_seed` |

The board's cells, undo history, and imported patterns are not stored in the settings
file. Use [workspace files](editing.qd#save-and-reopen-workspaces) to save your board
and imported library explicitly.

Settings save after 500 ms without further changes. Normal app exit, including window
close, saves pending changes immediately. The app writes a temporary file beside the
settings file and replaces the destination only after flushing the complete contents.
Failed writes retain the previous settings file and retry after a delay.

## The settings file

Settings are stored in `settings.toml`, in a `gol-bevy` folder in your platform's settings location:

| Platform | Folder                                                          |
|----------|-----------------------------------------------------------------|
| Linux    | `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gol-bevy`, or `~/.config/gol-bevy` if that is unset |
| macOS    | `~/Library/Application Support/gol-bevy`                        |
| Windows  | `%APPDATA%\gol-bevy`                                            |

Set the `GOL_BEVY_CONFIG_DIR` environment variable to use another folder on any platform. The file is then `$GOL_BEVY_CONFIG_DIR/settings.toml`, with no `gol-bevy` subfolder.

A typical file:

```toml "settings.toml"
board_cols = 256
board_rows = 256
fill_canvas = false
grid = false
cell_age = true
theme = "dark"
rule = "B3/S23"
boundary_mode = "wrap"
tick_rate_ms = 25
max_speed = false
random_density_percent = 25
# random_seed = 42  # Omit for a fresh seed each time.
```

| Key             | Values                                                    |
|-----------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| `board_cols`, `board_rows` | Whole numbers, clamped to 8–2048. Both must be present |
| `fill_canvas`   | `true` or `false`; with `true`, the saved size is replaced by the canvas's at startup |
| `grid`          | `true` or `false`                                         |
| `cell_age`      | `true` or `false`; `true` if missing                      |
| `theme`         | `"dark"`, `"light"`, or `"high-contrast"`                 |
| `rule`          | A [rulestring](simulation.qd#rulestring-formats)          |
| `boundary_mode` | `"wrap"` or `"fixed"`                                     |
| `tick_rate_ms`  | Milliseconds between generations, clamped to 25–1000      |
| `max_speed`     | `true` or `false`                                         |
| `random_density_percent` | Whole number from 0–100; 25 if missing or invalid |
| `random_seed` | Whole number from 0–18446744073709551615; omit for a fresh seed |

You can edit the file while the app is closed. Entries that are missing or can't be read keep their defaults, so a damaged file never stops the app from starting. Without a saved board size, the board is sized to fill the window.

### Moving from earlier versions

Earlier versions saved settings as `ui_layout.toml`, and before that in `~/.config/gol-bevy` on every platform. If `settings.toml` doesn't exist yet, gol-bevy reads `ui_layout.toml` from the same folder, or else from `~/.config/gol-bevy`, and the first save writes `settings.toml`. Old files are left alone, so you can delete them afterwards.

Old files may also hold `palette` or `layout_preset`. These are ignored now, since the palette follows the Stamp tool.
